\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Illustrative algebra of the halo-to-baryon circular-support coordinate, $X=V_h^2/V_{\rm  bar}^2$, for positive baryonic support and nonnegative residual support. (a) The residual fraction is $f_{\rm  supp}=X/(1+X)$. (b) The corresponding speed ratio is $V_{\rm  obs}/V_{\rm  bar}=\sqrt  {1+X}$. The marked count-selected coordinate $10/\pi $ maps to $f_{\rm  supp}\simeq 0.76094$ and a speed ratio of approximately $2.0453$. No galaxy data, empirical scatter, radial profile or fitted trend is shown. A support fraction is an enclosed mass fraction only under the geometry assumptions needed for that conversion.}}{4}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:c10:supportmapping}{{1}{4}{Illustrative algebra of the halo-to-baryon circular-support coordinate, $X=V_h^2/V_{\rm bar}^2$, for positive baryonic support and nonnegative residual support. (a) The residual fraction is $f_{\rm supp}=X/(1+X)$. (b) The corresponding speed ratio is $V_{\rm obs}/V_{\rm bar}=\sqrt {1+X}$. The marked count-selected coordinate $10/\pi $ maps to $f_{\rm supp}\simeq 0.76094$ and a speed ratio of approximately $2.0453$. No galaxy data, empirical scatter, radial profile or fitted trend is shown. A support fraction is an enclosed mass fraction only under the geometry assumptions needed for that conversion}{figure.caption.16}{}}
